 "pairing": {
  "paired_to_printed_page": 219,
  "state": "paired",
  "why": "RESOLVED by T-0642 where this says paired, and recorded as unpaired with its rule where it does not. The earlier text of this field said the left sheets had not been footed yet; they have now.",
  "page_population_key": 201,
  "line_count_key": 31,
  "paired_to_familysearch_id": "33S7-9YYJ-9K3",
  "tested_by": "T-0642",
  "test": "both keys — the count of ruled lines carrying an entry, and the page population — against the twelve filled left sheets of image group 1, whose printed footings T-0642 read and published.",
  "result": "The line counts agree at 31, and 33S7-9YYJ-9K3's own 31 lines hold 201 free white and free coloured persons - the same number this sheet's enumerator footed under TOTAL. No other left sheet in the group comes within eleven of 201 by either measure, so the match is unique as well as exact. 33S7-9YYJ-24 is the calibration sheet of the continuation reading and closes on all five of its own footed columns, which is what makes its 201 worth pairing on.",
  "what_it_settles": "33S7-9YYJ-9K3's disputed column 29. Its footing is two hooked diagonals, read by T-0585 as 11 against 4 in the column's own cells, and the sheet alone could not decide. If the footing were 11 the page would hold 208 persons and the facing continuation foots 201; with the footing read as 4 the two sheets agree exactly. That is a SECOND and INDEPENDENT witness for 4 - and it is the same two-stroke 4/11 form T-0627 and T-0645 settled toward 4 on 33S7-9YYJ-6H. This file does not rewrite the page file: the residual of 7 stays recorded there, with this pairing named beside it.",
  "published_in": "data/research/census_1840/left_sheet_population_key.json"
 },
